            answer="Assault and battery are separate torts that involve harmful or offensive contact. Assault requires an overt act that creates a reasonable apprehension of imminent harmful or offensive contact, while battery requires actual harmful or offensive contact. For example, if someone raises a fist to strike you but doesn't make contact, that's assault, but if they actually punch you, that's battery.",
