            deep_hint="Think about the scenario where a party holds something valuable that multiple people claim. Consider the conditions under which this legal tool can be used to resolve disputes.",
            answer="Interpleader is a legal procedure that allows a stakeholder, who is caught between competing claims, to force the claimants to litigate their dispute in a single action. It is available under Rule 22 of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure or through statutory interpleader under 28 U.S.C. § 1335, which requires minimal diversity between the claimants. A stakeholder is someone holding property or money that multiple parties claim, and interpleader helps avoid multiple lawsuits by consolidating the claims. For example, if a bank holds a check that two people both claim is theirs, the bank can use interpleader to force the two individuals to sue each other in one court instead of filing separate lawsuits.",
