            answer="The Pinkerton doctrine holds all members of a conspiracy liable for crimes committed by co-conspirators if those crimes were foreseeable and in furtherance of the conspiracy's goals. Conspiracy requires an agreement to commit an unlawful act, and liability extends to any foreseeable harm caused by actions taken to achieve the conspiracy's purpose. For example, if a group plans a bank robbery and one member instead robs a convenience store, all members could be liable if the store robbery was a foreseeable method to achieve their shared goal. Withdrawal from the conspiracy may limit liability, but only if the person actively prevents the crime and does not benefit from it.",
