            answer="The corpus delicti rule requires prosecutors to have independent evidence proving a crime occurred before a defendant's confession can be used as the sole basis for conviction. This ensures confessions are not the only proof, reducing risks of false confessions. Corpus delicti refers to the 'body of the crime'—the actual occurrence of the criminal act. Independent evidence means proof outside the confession, like physical evidence or witness testimony. The corroboration rule mandates this extra evidence to confirm the confession's truth. For example, if a suspect confesses to theft but no stolen property is found, the confession alone cannot secure a conviction without additional proof of the crime's occurrence.",
