            deep_hint="Think about two dimensions: how long the estate can last and what happens to the land when the holder dies. Ask which estate continues through inheritance and which one ends with a particular person's life, and consider how the land passes on after each.",
            answer="A fee simple absolute is an ownership interest in land that lasts forever, while a life estate lasts only for the duration of a person's life. A fee simple absolute gives the owner complete control and the right to pass the property to heirs, whereas a life estate ends when the life tenant dies, with the property passing to a remainderman or reverting to the original owner. For example, if someone owns a house in fee simple, they can sell it or pass it to their children, but if they hold a life estate, the property will transfer to another person upon their death. A remainder is the interest that follows a life estate, while a reversion is when the original owner gets the property back after the life estate ends. Waste refers to the misuse or damage of property by a life tenant, which can lead to legal consequences.",
