            deep_hint="This rule prevents future interests from lasting indefinitely by tying their duration to the lives of people currently alive. Think about why this limitation exists and how it affects the enforceability of long-term property rights.",
            answer="The Rule Against Perpetuities limits how long future interests in property can last, ensuring that property isn't controlled indefinitely by people who are already dead. It requires that any future interest must vest, if at all, within 21 years after the death of the last person alive at the time the interest was created. This rule is complex because it involves tracking 'lives in being' and applying a 'wait-and-see' approach to determine if an interest will vest within the allowed time. For example, if a will leaves property to someone's grandchildren, it must vest within 21 years after the death of the last surviving grandparent. The 'uniform statutory rule' replaces this common law rule in many jurisdictions, simplifying the application by setting a clear time limit for future interests to vest.",
