            answer="The 'waste' doctrine prevents life tenants from damaging property they don't own, protecting the landlord's future interest. Voluntary waste is intentional harm, like demolishing a building; permissive waste is neglect, like letting a roof collapse; ameliorative waste reduces the property's value, such as removing fixtures. A life tenant holds property for their lifetime but can't diminish its value for the landlord (remainderman). Fixtures—like built-in appliances—are considered part of the land, so removing them without permission counts as ameliorative waste. For example, if a life tenant removes a custom kitchen to sell it, they harm the landlord's future value, triggering a legal claim.",
