            answer="The 'business records' hearsay exception (FRE 803(6)) allows regularly kept business records to be admitted as evidence even if they are hearsay. This applies when the record was made in the regular course of business, by someone with knowledge, and it was kept as part of the business's routine. The custodian of records (the person responsible for maintaining them) must testify to their existence and regular use. Trustworthiness is assumed because businesses are presumed to keep accurate records, but this exception doesn’t apply if the records lack trustworthiness (e.g., if the business has a history of falsifying documents). For example, a restaurant’s receipt showing a customer’s purchase could be admitted to prove the transaction occurred, as long as the receipt was routinely created and the owner (custodian) confirms its authenticity.",
